{
  id: "r5", title: "The Myth of the Lone Decipherer", difficulty: "7.0-8.0", timeMinutes: 20,
  paragraphs: [
    { label: "A", text: "The decipherment of an ancient script has long held a particular place in the popular imagination. We picture a solitary genius, surrounded by clay tablets, who one night sees a pattern that has eluded everyone else. I have come to think this picture is not merely romantic but actively misleading, because it hides the slow, collective and often unglamorous nature of the work. Nearly every successful decipherment rests on a foundation laid by people whose names are rarely remembered." },
    { label: "B", text: "Consider the Rosetta Stone, discovered in 1799. Because it carried the same decree in hieroglyphic, a later Egyptian script and Greek, scholars had a parallel text to work from, and the early progress made by Thomas Young was considerable. It was Jean-François Champollion who, in the 1820s, showed that hieroglyphs were not purely symbolic, as had been assumed for centuries, but combined signs representing sounds with signs representing ideas. His knowledge of Coptic, a descendant of the ancient Egyptian tongue, proved crucial, since it allowed him to guess how the words might have been pronounced. Without the stone, and without Coptic, it is doubtful that he would have succeeded." },
    { label: "C", text: "The case of Linear B is even more instructive. This script, found on clay tablets at Cretan and mainland Greek sites, was cracked in 1952 by Michael Ventris, an architect who had pursued the problem as a hobby since adolescence. Ventris is often presented as an amateur who triumphed where professionals failed, but this story does him less than justice and ignores others. Years earlier, the American scholar Alice Kober had shown that the signs behaved in a way that suggested a language with inflected endings, and she had constructed painstaking tables of the patterns. She died in 1950, before she could use them. Ventris, who had initially believed the language to be related to Etruscan, was surprised to find that his grids pointed to an early form of Greek. His achievement was real; so was hers, and it is regrettable that it is so seldom acknowledged." },
    { label: "D", text: "Why have some scripts yielded while others have not? Success appears to depend on a few conditions: a reasonable quantity of text, some knowledge of the underlying language or a close relative, and ideally a bilingual inscription. Linear A, the earlier Cretan script, has survived in only a small number of short inscriptions, and the language it recorded is unknown. The signs of the Indus Valley civilisation are found on thousands of seals, but most inscriptions consist of only a handful of symbols, and no bilingual text has been found. Under such circumstances, even the cleverest method has little to work with." },
    { label: "E", text: "Computing has been hailed as the answer. In recent years, researchers have developed programs that, given a script and a related language, can propose likely word equivalences automatically; one such system correctly matched most of the vocabulary of an ancient language of the Syrian coast to Hebrew. These results are impressive, but I am not convinced that they amount to deciphering in the full sense. The software confirmed what scholars already knew, and it depended on a known relative. For the truly unknown scripts, no such relative exists, and an algorithm cannot invent evidence that the ground has not provided." },
    { label: "F", text: "A more fundamental question concerns whether certain sets of symbols represent language at all. In 2004, three researchers argued that the Indus signs might be a system of non-linguistic emblems, comparable to heraldic badges, and the claim provoked a vigorous reaction. Critics produced statistical analyses suggesting that the sequences show the regularities typical of language. I find it difficult to decide between the two positions on present evidence, but I do believe the sceptics performed a service by reminding the field how easily scholars assume that any set of marks must be writing." },
    { label: "G", text: "Announcements of decipherments appear regularly in the press, and few survive scrutiny. The island script known as rongorongo, for instance, has attracted numerous confident claims, none of which has won general acceptance. What would genuinely help, in my view, is not a flash of inspiration but a better supply of data: high-quality photographs published openly, comprehensive catalogues, and cooperation between scholars rather than rivalry. It is a much less exciting prescription than the myth of the lone genius, but it is the one that history supports." }
  ],
  groups: [
    {
      type: "ynng",
      instructions: "Do the following statements agree with the claims of the writer in the passage? Write YES, NO or NOT GIVEN.",
      questions: [
        { n: 1, prompt: "The writer believes that the popular image of the solitary decipherer gives a false impression of how scripts are decoded.", answer: "YES", explanation: "O autor diz que a imagem é 'not merely romantic but actively misleading, because it hides the slow, collective ... nature of the work'.", tip: "'False impression' é paráfrase de 'misleading'; a opinião do autor está frequentemente numa expressão avaliativa forte." },
        { n: 2, prompt: "The writer thinks the Rosetta Stone on its own was sufficient for Champollion's success.", answer: "NO", explanation: "O autor escreve que 'Without the stone, and without Coptic, it is doubtful that he would have succeeded', logo a pedra não bastou.", tip: "'On its own' é a armadilha: o texto aponta dois fatores necessários, não um." },
        { n: 3, prompt: "The writer considers that Alice Kober has not received the recognition she deserves.", answer: "YES", explanation: "O autor lamenta: 'it is regrettable that it is so seldom acknowledged'.", tip: "'Regrettable' sinaliza opinião; 'seldom acknowledged' = reconhecimento insuficiente." },
        { n: 4, prompt: "Ventris was disappointed that his grids pointed to a form of Greek.", answer: "NOT GIVEN", explanation: "O texto diz que ele ficou 'surprised' ao descobrir o grego, mas nada afirma sobre desilusão.", tip: "Surpresa não é o mesmo que desilusão; não infira emoções que o texto não nomeia." },
        { n: 5, prompt: "The writer accepts that computer programs have produced some impressive results in decipherment.", answer: "YES", explanation: "O autor admite: 'These results are impressive', embora duvide de que sejam decifração em sentido pleno.", tip: "Concessões ('impressive, but...') contam: a parte concessiva é uma opinião real do autor." },
        { n: 6, prompt: "The writer believes that the 2004 claim about the Indus signs did the field no good.", answer: "NO", explanation: "O autor afirma que 'the sceptics performed a service by reminding the field how easily scholars assume that any set of marks must be writing'.", tip: "'Did no good' contradiz 'performed a service'; atenção a negações no enunciado." }
      ]
    },
    {
      type: "gap",
      instructions: "Complete the summary below. Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS from the passage for each answer.",
      questions: [
        { n: 7, prompt: "According to the writer, a script is more likely to be deciphered if there is a reasonable quantity of ___ available.", answer: "text", accept: ["text"], explanation: "O parágrafo D indica como condição 'a reasonable quantity of text'.", tip: "A palavra 'reasonable' no enunciado é âncora literal: procure-a no texto." },
        { n: 8, prompt: "It also helps if scholars know the underlying language or a ___ of it.", answer: "close relative", accept: ["close relative"], explanation: "O texto diz 'some knowledge of the underlying language or a close relative'.", tip: "Duas palavras, adjetivo + substantivo; confirme que cabem no limite." },
        { n: 9, prompt: "Ideally there should be a ___ inscription.", answer: "bilingual", accept: ["bilingual"], explanation: "A frase relevante é 'and ideally a bilingual inscription'.", tip: "Pelo posicionamento antes de 'inscription', a lacuna pede um adjetivo." },
        { n: 10, prompt: "Linear A has survived in only a small number of ___ inscriptions.", answer: "short", accept: ["short"], explanation: "O texto diz que Linear A 'has survived in only a small number of short inscriptions'.", tip: "Copie a palavra exata do texto; sinónimos como 'brief' não são aceites." }
      ]
    },
    {
      type: "mcq",
      instructions: "Choose the correct letter, A, B, C or D.",
      questions: [
        { n: 11, prompt: "Why does the writer mention Coptic in paragraph B?", options: ["A It was the language written on the Rosetta Stone.", "B It helped Champollion work out how words were pronounced.", "C It had been studied extensively by Thomas Young.", "D It was the oldest known form of Egyptian."], answer: "B", explanation: "O texto explica que o copta 'allowed him to guess how the words might have been pronounced'.", tip: "Pergunte 'porquê' e procure a função da informação, não apenas a menção." },
        { n: 12, prompt: "What is the writer's view of the computer programs described in paragraph E?", options: ["A They are too slow to be of practical use.", "B They depend on a language relative that is already known.", "C They have already deciphered the Indus script.", "D They will soon make human scholars unnecessary."], answer: "B", explanation: "O autor sublinha que o software 'depended on a known relative' e que para escritas desconhecidas 'no such relative exists'.", tip: "Opções com afirmações absolutas (C, D) raramente correspondem a um autor cauteloso." },
        { n: 13, prompt: "What does the writer recommend as the best way to make progress?", options: ["A Waiting for a gifted individual to find a new method", "B Encouraging competition between rival scholars", "C Making data widely available and working together", "D Concentrating on scripts that have a bilingual text"], answer: "C", explanation: "A recomendação final é 'high-quality photographs published openly, comprehensive catalogues, and cooperation between scholars rather than rivalry'.", tip: "A conclusão do texto costuma conter a resposta a perguntas sobre recomendação." }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
